  "slug": "microbiome-metagenomics-9f55",
  "summary": "New preprints show long-read assembly, MAG recovery and pangenomics changing which taxa and functions are inferred across sludge, sediment and polar ocean systems.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is that resolution of genomic and phylogenetic structure shapes ecological interpretation. HiFi long-read metagenomics of activated sludge recovers denitrifiers, nitrifiers and Accumulibacter missed by short-read assemblies, biasing N2O-related inferences. Great Barrier Reef sediments yield 2,698 MAGs, mostly novel, with notable metabolic heterogeneity. The Micromonas polaris pangenome resolves two cryptic ecotypes and 990 polar-specific genes, and metaEukaryome detects eukaryotic bycatch in 75,727 metagenomes. Amplicon-based studies add context at coarser resolution: marble biofilms show taxonomic turnover with conserved phylogenetic structure, river microeukaryotes track water chemistry, and octocoral microbiomes are host-specific and stable over three years. The systems and methods differ, and only some of the work is genome-resolved.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-24"
}
